Dear INLA experts,

I have recently been using the 'slm'  model to model the over-dispersed spatial counting data. I originally thought that using nbinomial and ZIP  likelihood would be better than Poisson likelihood, but the results are not what I thought. Do you have any suggestions for me to achieve the desired results. Thank you

From u66ed4f72a898a51c at groups.google.com  Mon Dec 26 08:09:01 2022
From: u66ed4f72a898a51c at groups.google.com (Elias T. Krainski)
Date: Mon, 26 Dec 2022 08:09:01 +0000
Subject: Re: [r-inla] over-dispersed spatial counting data
Message-ID: <eCDvV1ccwVk.IGPBc7MfBwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <eCDvV1ccwVk.KkQzYcbVBgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
References: <eCDvV1ccwVk.KkQzYcbVBgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/eCDvV1ccwVk/m/IGPBc7MfBwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi,

The nbinomial and ZIP can be seen as two, different, extensions of a Poisson variable Y for a given lambda. However, if you use Poisson and consider lambda varying with some random effect and this random effect is at the level of each observation, this is better as you can check which observations have extra variability. This is towards trying to explain/model overdispersion directly instead of marginally doing with some Poisson extension to account for. Maybe the 'slm' model already explains the overdispersion and extending Poisson to nbinomial or ZIP does not add. Please consider a simpler example here: https://haakonbakkagit.github.io/btopic125.html

best regards,
Elias

